        </div><div class="article-template__content page-width page-width--narrow rte scroll-trigger animate--slide-in">
          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        The best sunscreen for oily skin is one you will actually wear every day: broad-spectrum, SPF 30 or higher and labelled non-comedogenic, in a texture that stays light through the day.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Choose a broad-spectrum sunscreen with SPF 30 or higher, water resistance and a non-comedogenic label, in a gel, fluid or light lotion that feels comfortable on oily skin. If you also have dark marks, a tinted formula with iron oxide adds protection against visible light. The amount you apply and a reapplication matter as much as the product you pick.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Oily skin tends to skip sunscreen because so many formulas feel heavy, shiny or greasy by afternoon. That is a real problem, because a sunscreen you do not wear protects nothing. The goal is to find a formula light enough that putting it on every morning feels easy.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What to check on the label
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Five details on the pack tell you most of what you need to know:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Broad-spectrum.</strong> This means protection against both UVA and UVB rays.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>SPF 30 or higher.</strong> Dermatologists recommend this minimum along with water resistance, which helps when you sweat.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Non-comedogenic.</strong> For oily skin, dermatologists specifically suggest a non-comedogenic formula, which is designed not to clog pores.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>A shade that blends in.</strong> Dermatologists suggest a formula that blends with your skin tone, so it does not leave a white cast.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Iron oxide, if you have dark marks.</strong> Iron oxide protects against visible light, which is a cause of dark spots, and it appears in the inactive ingredients list because only UV filters count as active ingredients.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a>
</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        None of this depends on price. A drugstore gel that checks all five boxes and gets worn daily will do more for your skin than an expensive one that stays in the drawer.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Texture matters more on oily skin
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Researchers who reviewed sunscreens for acne-prone skin suggest that the best formulas have emollient, antioxidant and sebum-controlling properties.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36946555/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a> The same review found that sunscreen can help prevent the dark marks that acne leaves behind, which is one more reason to keep wearing it.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        In practice, textures fall into three groups:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Gels and fluids.</strong> The lightest options, and usually the easiest to wear on hot or humid days.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Light lotions.</strong> A little more cushioning, which suits combination skin with dry cheeks.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Tinted fluids.</strong> Useful when you have dark marks to protect, since the iron oxide in the tint covers visible light.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a>
</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Test a new sunscreen on your cheek and jaw for a few days before committing to it. If it feels greasy by midday, try a lighter texture in place of skipping it.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            A sunscreen that breaks you out or feels too heavy is a formula problem, not a reason to stop wearing sunscreen. Switch to a lighter, non-comedogenic option and see a dermatologist if breakouts continue.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        How much to apply and when
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Sunscreen is tested at a generous layer of 2 mg per square centimetre. Studies show that people apply only about 0.39 to 1.0 mg per square centimetre in real life, which lowers the protection they actually get.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24313722/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a> Applying enough is the single biggest upgrade you can make.
    </p>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Apply before you go out.</strong> Sunscreen takes about 15 minutes to absorb and protect.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Use enough for your face.</strong> Dermatologists suggest at least 1 teaspoon, which is about the length of your index and middle fingers.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Make it the last step of your morning routine.</strong> Apply it after your moisturiser.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Reapply every two hours outdoors,</strong> and straight after sweating or swimming.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
    </ol>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        On oily skin, dab off excess shine with a tissue first and then reapply on top. Researchers also note that a quick reapplication, or a very high SPF, can partly make up for applying less than the tested amount, though it is no substitute for using enough in the first place.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24313722/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a>
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Dark marks, melasma and visible light
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Ultraviolet rays are only part of the story. Visible light can also darken skin, and it causes pigmentation in people with darker skin tones.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a> A regular broad-spectrum sunscreen protects against UV but does not adequately cover visible light. To protect against it, a sunscreen has to be visible on the skin, which is why tinted formulas with iron oxide and pigmentary titanium dioxide are the ones that help.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a>
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        This matters most if you are dealing with dark marks after acne or with melasma. Dermatologists say that sunscreen can prevent new dark spots and help clear existing ones, and that a spot a few shades darker than your natural colour usually fades within 6 to 12 months once its cause is stopped.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> Sunscreen is what keeps new marks from forming while the old ones fade.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        A simple texture guide
    </h2>

    <div style="margin:24px 0 34px;overflow-x:auto;border-top:2px solid #181818;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);">
        <table style="width:100%;min-width:620px;border-collapse:collapse;text-align:left;font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;">
            <thead>
                <tr style="background:#FFFEF3;">
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Option</th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Best suited to</th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">What to check</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Gel</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Oily skin, hot or humid days</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Broad-spectrum and water-resistant on the label</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Fluid or light lotion</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Combination skin, daily office wear</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">A non-comedogenic label and SPF 30 or higher<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;font-weight:700;">Tinted fluid</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Oily skin with dark marks</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Iron oxide in the inactive ingredients<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        A light morning routine
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Oily skin does best with a short routine, because every extra layer is another chance for shine and congestion. A simple version looks like this:
    </p>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Cleanse</strong> with a gentle cleanser that leaves skin comfortable, not squeaky.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Hydrate</strong> with a light serum or gel moisturiser, because oily skin can still be short on water.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Finish with sunscreen,</strong> using the full amount, so everything underneath stays protected.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Common mistakes with oily skin
    </h2>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Skipping it because skin is already shiny.</strong> Oil does not block UV, and your skin is exposed whenever you go outside, even on cloudy days.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Using too little to avoid the greasy feeling.</strong> A thin layer gives less than the SPF on the label.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24313722/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Applying once and forgetting.</strong> A morning layer wears off over the day, so reapply when you are outdoors.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Choosing by SPF number alone.</strong> A higher number helps, but amount, reapplication and a texture you enjoy matter just as much.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24313722/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a>
</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>

    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can oily skin skip sunscreen?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No. Oiliness does not protect you from UV rays, and your skin is exposed every time you go outside, even on cloudy days.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is a tinted sunscreen worth it for oily skin?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">It is worth it if you have dark marks or melasma. Iron oxide protects against visible light, which untinted sunscreens do not cover well, and dermatologists recommend tinted sunscreen with iron oxide for caring for dark spots.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How much sunscreen should I use on my face?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">At least 1 teaspoon, which is roughly the length of your index and middle fingers.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a> Use less and you get less protection than the label promises.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24313722/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Will sunscreen make me break out?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Choosing a non-comedogenic formula, which is designed not to clog pores, lowers the chance.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> If breakouts continue with a light formula, see a dermatologist, because sun exposure itself can also worsen acne.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36946555/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is SPF 50 better than SPF 30 for oily skin?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Dermatologists recommend SPF 30 or higher.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a> A higher SPF can partly make up for applying too little, but it does not replace using the right amount and reapplying.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24313722/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">What if my sunscreen leaves a white cast?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Look for a formula that blends with your skin tone.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> Tinted sunscreens come in many shades made to suit different skin tones, and the tint also helps protect against visible light.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

